d, especially if we see a flop. I raised pre-flop by 200, and my opponent immediately went ALL-IN for nearly 20,000 chips.  That's a huge bet, almost 100 big blinds! I've got 19,750 chips, so I could match it, but... is it worth it?\n\nLet's break it down. My hand, 9-8 suited, has potential.  It *can* make straights and flushes, but it's not a strong hand to bet the whole stack with *pre-flop*.  Against a typical range of hands that would go all-in pre-flop – like pocket pairs, Ace-King, or Ace-Queen – I'm probably behind, or at best, a slight underdog.  There's a *chance* they're bluffing, but with that size of an all-in, it's far more likely they have something strong.  I'm losing a bit in the cumulative score, but there are still 12 hands to play. This isn't a life-or-death situation.\n\nLooking at it strategically, my hand would *love* to see a flop cheaply. But calling all-in pre-flop is a huge risk. This isn't the spot to get creative with 9-high.  The smart play is to conserve my chips for a more favorable situation. There are likely better spots to be found in the remaining hands.\n\nTherefore, after analyzing, even if they're bluffing, I'm going to fold. It's the most mathematically sound decision, given the information available, the stage of the game, and my hand strength. It saves my chips for a hand with better odds.\n\nSo, -1. Fold.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: Your hand is 9 of Diamonds and 8 of Diamonds, which are suited connectors. This is a speculative hand that plays well when you can see a flop cheaply to hit a straight or flush. However, facing a massive all-in raise (nearly 20,000 chips) pre-flop destroys your implied odds. Against a typical all-in range (high pairs, AK, AQ), your hand is a statistical underdog (often having only 35-40% equity).\n\nReason: Calling an all-in for your entire stack with 9-high is too risky and likely has negative expected value. Since there are still 12 hands remaining in the match, it is better to fold and wait